+# Profile save and failure recovery
+
+## Contract and decision
+
+`README.md` defines a concurrent, optimistic edit flow. The draft is editable without
+saving. Each submit snapshots that draft, immediately publishes the submitted name,
+and calls the existing PATCH endpoint. An older response must not replace the latest
+submitted name. A rejected latest save displays the confirmed fallback and the
+accessible message `Save failed. Please retry.`; the draft remains available to edit
+or submit again. Successful responses can normalize the submitted name.
+
+The API boundary must reject unsuccessful HTTP responses, invalid JSON, and bodies
+without string `id`/`displayName` and numeric `version`. The public `Profile` type,
+`updateProfile(displayName: string): Promise<Profile>` and
+`ProfileForm({ profile }: { profile: Profile })` entry points stay intact.
+
+Use local submission identities for UI precedence. An acknowledged save updates
+the fallback only if it is a later submission than the previously acknowledged
+save. This preserves newer accepted user intent against delayed older responses.
+This is a client presentation policy, not a statement about server commit order.
+The repository gives no monotonicity contract for `version`; preserve and validate
+it, but do not rank versions. There is no refresh/conflict endpoint in scope, so
+cross-client or server write ordering cannot be guaranteed here.
+
+The existing React state and fetch entry points are sufficient. Use a local pure
+reducer for atomic acknowledgement/failure transitions and a ref only for allocating
+request identities. The draft remains ordinary input state. A general cache, request
+engine, cancellation policy, dependency container or state-machine library adds no
+needed capability. In particular, do not cancel accepted older saves: they may
+become the latest available confirmed fallback.
+
+## Implementation sequence (2 completed steps)
+
+1. Strengthen `src/api.ts` at the transport trust boundary. `updateProfile` owns
+   PATCH construction, HTTP status checking and response decoding. It resolves only
+   with a validated `Profile`, otherwise rejects. Check HTTP failure, invalid JSON,
+   invalid field types and a valid normalized response through the actual form.
+2. Complete `src/ProfileForm.tsx`. `handleSubmit(event: FormEvent)` snapshots the
+   draft, dispatches `submitted`, executes `updateProfile`, and dispatches either
+   `confirmed` or `failed`. `reduceSave(state: SaveState, event: SaveEvent): SaveState`
+   owns the latest submission and highest acknowledged submission. The component
+   derives current name and alert from that state. Check pending optimism, draft
+   preservation, success normalization, overlapping results in both arrival orders,
+   rollback to an earlier accepted save and retry via the existing Save button.
+
+## State and flow
+
+State consists of the confirmed profile with its submission ID, plus the latest
+submission (`pending`, `confirmed` or `failed`) with its captured name and ID. On
+submit, only latest intent changes. Every success may advance the confirmed
+fallback; only success for the latest submission ends its pending state. Failure
+changes visible status only when it belongs to the latest submission. Current name
+is the latest captured name while pending, and the confirmed fallback otherwise.
+Late success for an earlier request after latest failure may still advance the
+fallback. Late success/error after newer success must leave that success visible.
+No async response mutates the draft.
+
+Architecture (plain text, runtime/dependency arrows):
+
+```text
+React ProfileForm
+  ├─ owns draft + request identity allocation
+  ├─ dispatches SaveEvent ──> local reduceSave ──> SaveState ──> name/alert
+  └─ calls updateProfile(name) ──> fetch PATCH /api/profile [server boundary]
+                                  └─ validates status + body ──> Profile / rejection
+```
+
+Data flow (including overlap):
+
+```text
+edit draft ──> submit A ──> publish A + PATCH A
+edit draft ──> submit B ──> publish B + PATCH B
+                         A confirmed ──> advance fallback; keep pending B visible
+                         B rejected  ──> publish current fallback + alert
+                         B confirmed ──> advance fallback; publish returned B
+                         stale A failure/success after B confirmed ──> keep B
+retry Save ──> new submission of retained/editable draft ──> clear alert + PATCH
+```
+
+React owns view lifetime; completion only dispatches to that mounted form instance.
+No shared store, subscriptions, background retry or external resource is introduced.
+No performance change or browser-layout claim is intended.
+
+## Verification and maintenance
+
+Run the existing `npm run check` unchanged and add separate consumer regressions.
+The original tests omit explicit React Testing Library cleanup and this repository
+does not enable Vitest globals, so automatic cleanup is inactive. A shared
+`src/testSetup.ts`, registered by `vitest.config.ts`, performs `afterEach(cleanup)`
+without editing the protected tests or adding test-only behavior to the form.
+Raw baseline/final command output is kept in the workspace's sibling
+`frontend-architect-autonomous-live-profile-candidate-42bbov1a-validation.log`.
+Executed on 2026-11-12: `npm run check` passes TypeScript and all 15 tests across
+the existing and additional consumer test files. The first attempt could not find
+`tsc`; `npm ci` installed the exact locked dependencies. The next run exposed the
+cleanup issue above; the final unchanged check command passes after adding shared
+test cleanup. Protected `package.json`, `package-lock.json` and the original test
+have unchanged SHA-256 hashes. Checks run in jsdom with controlled fetch promises;
+no live server, real-browser layout or server write-order guarantee was exercised.
+
+Final code/record cross-check: `reduceSave` is private in `ProfileForm.tsx` and
+receives the documented `SaveState`/`SaveEvent`; `handleSubmit` is owned inline by
+`ProfileForm` and calls the existing `updateProfile`. The derived `visibleName` and
+`role="alert"` consume reducer state, and API decoding completes before any
+`confirmed` event. These definitions and callers implement both flow diagrams.
+
+A realistic next change is adding a second editable field. Keep its draft in the
+form and submit the complete profile snapshot; acknowledgement ordering stays in
+the reducer. The private reducer separates that transition policy from transport
+decoding and input rendering without creating a universal form controller.
